1. registering the source interest;
2. serving source rows from cache/store;
3. marking the source dirty when relevant events/account state change;
4. running a deterministic reducer;
5. diffing previous vs. next reduced output;
6. closing removed targets;
7. opening changed/new targets through existing registries;
8. triggering subscription recompilation.
 [...] - Account switch re-resolves active-account sources and closes stale old-account targets.
- Relay reroute/NIP-65 changes recompile materialized interests without re-declaring the source.
- Cache-first/offline behavior must reduce from stored source events before relay results arrive.
- Recompute must be actor/kernel-owned, event-driven, and non-polling.
- Large outputs must be partitioned by existing planner/wire constraints, not silently capped.
- Native shells must never receive or compute the concrete expanded source set for correctness. [...] - Account login, logout, account switch, follow/unfollow, source replacement, source deletion, and relay reroute all close stale targets and open current targets.
- Missing source data fails closed and keeps the source interest live.
